<article>
    <h1>Please make a Doom map</h1>
    <p>
        <label>Created:</label> 2026-09-06<br />
        <label>Author:</label> J.M. "JM" Janzen<br />
    </p>

    <p>
        You don't have to listen to me (I free you), but Doom mapping
        is something I enjoy a lot, so if it's something that sounds like it
        would be even a little interesting to you, I strongly encourage you to
        try.
    </p>
    <p>
        Here, looks at these screenshots and tell me it doesn't stir some primal generative urge
        within you.
    </p>

    <section class="image-strip">
        <a href="/static/img/udb-ui.jpg">
            <img
                src="/static/img/udb-ui.thumb.jpg"
                alt="Ultimate Doombuilder UI --- top-down perspective showing a map composed mostly
                of right angles with medium density of monsters">
        </a>
        <a href="/static/img/doom-combi.jpg">
            <img
                src="/static/img/doom-combi.thumb.jpg"
                alt="Doom FPS screenshot --- first-person perspective showing dark grey hallway with
                thin stripes of lighting leading to a door marked with an 'exit' sign">
        </a>
        <p>
            <em>Screenshots of a map being edited and playtested</em>
        </p>
    </section>
    <p>
        Jump to <a href="#tldr">Taggart, Let's Doom Run</a> for the short
        version.
    </p>

    <h2>Step 1: "But can it run Doom?"</h1>
    <p>
        So, you will need to actually be able to <em>run</em> Doom to get anything out of this, so if you aren't set up to do that yet, here are some basic instructions. (Note, I default to linux because that's what I run, but other <span title="😒">proprietary</span> "operating systems" should also be able to run it.)
    </p>

    <h3>Step 1.1: Install some Doom</h3>
    <p>
        If you already have a version of Doom purchased to play on a computer,
        then you just need to find the file named <code>DOOM.WAD</code> to make
        a map for Doom 1 (1993), or <code>DOOM2.WAD</code> to make a map of
        Jazz Jackrabbit 2 (1994).
    </p>

    <details>
        <summary>
            [ Aside into sourceport and WAD specifics ... ]
        </summary>
        <p>
            These WAD files (short of "Where's All the Data" (weren't the 90's
            something?)) contain the <em>assets</em> (Eg. the textures, monsters,
            maps, etc) that show up in the game when you run Doom, which is
            separate from the <em>game engine</em> that takes these assets and lets
            you run them in a playable form on your computer.
        </p>
        <p>
            Depending on how you purchased your copy of Doom, these <code>.WAD</code> files could be in various locations on your PC or installation CD. So if you can't find it by searching your filesystem, run a web search for "where is DOOM.WAD file {GoG,Steam,CD,floppy,etc}"
        </p>
        <p>
            Nowadays these game engines are knows as "source ports" (ports of the
            source code of the original Doom game engine). That's the somewhat
            accurate abridged version anyway. The point is there are <a
            href="https://doomwiki.org/wiki/Source_port">a lot</a> of source ports
            available, free and legal.
        </p>
    </details>

    <p>
        To make things simpler for you, I'm going to suggest you use <a
        href="https://www.chocolate-doom.org/wiki/index.php/Chocolate_Doom">Chocolate
        Doom</a> if you're looking for a more authentic retro experience, and
        <a href="https://zdoom.org/downloads">UZDoom</a> if you want a flashier
        experience.
    </p>

    <h3>Step 1.1b: But I have no Doom and no money!</h3>
    <p>
        If you don't have a copy of Doom to copy a <code>.WAD</code> from, then
        no problem! You can achieve the same results using <a
        href="https://freedoom.github.io/download.html">FREEDOOM</a>, and just
        subsituting <code>freedom.wad</code> or <code>freedoom2.wad</code> for
        the above. The assets will appear different, but you'll be able to make
        and play maps just as easily.
    </p>

    <h2>Step 2: Doombuilding</h2>
    <p>
        Now that you've got some form of Doom up and running on your computer,
        it's time to <strong>build</strong>. Some Doom. Doom-build, if you
        will.
    </p>
    <p>
        Like with the sourceports, there are pleth/ora (and a lot) of tools to
        make Doom levels (AKA "maps") to run using your assets and sourceport.
        Currently, the main map maping/editing utility is probably <a
        href="https://www.doomwiki.org/wiki/Ultimate_Doom_Builder">Ulimate&nbsp;Doombuilder</a>,
        so that's what I'll focus on. But there are a bunch of support tools to
        get into once you've got the basics down.
    </p>
    <p>
        So UDB, eh? OK bigdog, depending on your OS and your preferences, you
        can install this <a
        href="https://github.com/UltimateDoomBuilder/UltimateDoomBuilder#manual-build">from
        source</a>, via <a
        href="https://github.com/UltimateDoomBuilder/UltimateDoomBuilder/releases">handy
        flatpak</a>, and supposedly even this <a
        href="https://www.doomworld.com/forum/topic/158664-ultimate-bun-builder-udb-appimage-for-linux-users/">AppImage</a>
        (which I haven't tried out yet myself). There are also these <a
        href="https://ultimatedoombuilder.github.io/">other installers</a>, but
        idk anything about those.
    </p>
    <aside>
        (So quick disclaimer: I've been using some modified key binding in UDB for so many years
        that I forget what's original and what's my own edit. So if a keyboard shortcut isn't
        working for you, double check in <code>Tools &rarr; Preferences &rarr; Controls</code> and
        search for the functionality that you're missing. I'll endeavour to use correct or at least
        semantic terms, but you may have to do some scrolling, sorry.)
    </aside>

    <h3>Step 2.1: Make a box</h3>
    <p>
        So the bigshot has their Doom and their Doombuilder running. OK, let's see you put 'em together.
    </p>
    <section class="image-strip">
        <!-- FIXME Retake this screenshot with Doom 1 format selected (lol you're never doing
        this) -->
        <a href="/static/img/doom-udb-newmap.jpg">
            <img src="/static/img/doom-udb-newmap.thumb.jpg" alt="UDB Map Options modal with
            Doom 2 format selected, though I end up using Doom 1 format">
        </a>
        <a href="/static/img/doom-udb-jazz-jackrabbit.jpg">
            <img src="/static/img/doom-udb-jazz-jackrabbit.thumb.jpg" alt="Map Options, Add
            Resource,and Browse WAD File modals open, with DOOM.WAD selected in file browser">
        </a>
        <p>
            <em>Starting a new map file</em>
        </p>
    </section>
    <p>
        Launch your UDB and start a new map (<code>ctrl+n</code>). Game configuration dictates
        what features UDB will make available. For simplicity just pick a "Doom format"
        configuration matching the version of Doom you want to make a map for. So if you're
        mapping for Doom (1993) use <code>Doom: Doom (Doom format)</code>, and if you're
        mapping for Doom 2 (<span title="seriously, they could have cooked Doom 2 for way
        longer---probably should have">1994</span>) then you can use <code>Doom: Doom 2
        (Jazz&nbsp;Jackrabbit format)</code>.
    </p>
    <p>
        You'll also need to select the matching iWAD that contains all the textures and
        creatures that you'll be using (Doom or Jazz Jackrabbit or Doom 2, I guess).
    </p>

    <section class="image-strip">
        <a href="/static/img/doom-udb-draw-box-start.jpg">
            <img src="/static/img/doom-udb-draw-box-start.thumb.jpg" alt="UDB top-down grid view
            showing a partially drawn box, three lines are completed">
        </a>
        <a href="/static/img/doom-udb-draw-box-done.jpg">
            <img src="/static/img/doom-udb-draw-box-done.thumb.jpg" alt="UDB top-down grid vew
            showing a completed box, solid with a brown floor">
        </a>
        <p>
            <em>Drawing a sector</em>
        </p>
    </section>
    <p>
        Enter draw lines mode (<code title="maybe it's something else---I could have modified the
        default key bindings and forgotten">ctrl+d</code>) and just click around until you
        complete a box, or other shape. You shouldn't get in the habit of just drawing boring
        symmetrical boxes, but it's OK for this once.
    </p>

    <h3>Step 2.2: Put a little guy in the box</h3>
    <p>
        This would be a good time to save your map. It's pretty intuitive if you've used any
        graphical user interface in the last 30 years. I believe in you.
    </p>
    <p>
        (If you want to go ahead and try running your box now, see <a
        href="#cyrax-was-a-cool-character">Doomrunning</a>. It's not a bad habit to frequently
        playtest your maps as you're creating them.)
    </p>

    <section class="image-strip">
        <a href="/static/img/doom-udb-player-start.jpg">
            <img src="/static/img/doom-udb-player-start.thumb.jpg" alt="UDB top-down grid view
            showing a player sprite placed inside the previously drawn box">
        </a>
        <p>
            <em>Player 1 Thing</em>
        </p>
    </section>
    <p>
        Enter Things Mode (<code title="yeah, just T">t</code>) and <strong>right</strong>-click anywhere in your box.
        I think by default an "Edit Thing" modal will pop up with the Thing "Player 1 start"
        selected. Anyway, that is what you want.
    </p>

    <h3>Step 2.3: Let me out of the box!</h3>
    <p>
        So you've got a map, but you can't actually finish it. You just spawn in your box and run
        around until you decide to exit. So let's add an exit switch.
    </p>
    <p>
        Personally, I like to add an exit---even a temporary one---as soon as possible. Styles will
        vary, but it's my guide so I'm showing you my style. Anyway, here's how you do that.
    </p>

    <section class="image-strip">
        <a href="/static/img/doom-udb-exit-vertexes.jpg">
            <img src="/static/img/doom-udb-exit-vertexes.thumb.jpg" alt="UDB top-down grid view
            showing zoomed in perspective of south wall of the box with two points in the line">
        </a>
        <a href="/static/img/doom-udb-exit-edit-linedef.jpg">
            <img src="/static/img/doom-udb-exit-edit-linedef.thumb.jpg" alt="UDB first-person view
            showing player, small highlighted section of the south wall, and a modal with many
            linedef options">
        </a>
        <a href="/static/img/doom-udb-exit-browse-textures.jpg">
            <img src="/static/img/doom-udb-exit-browse-textures.thumb.jpg" alt="UDB first-person
            view showing texture selection modal with exit switch highlighted">
        </a>
        <p>
            <em>Making the exit switch</em>
        </p>
    </section>
    <p>
        Enter Vertices mode (<code>v</code>) and <strong>right</strong>-click on one of the of your
        box to make a smaller sub-line that is 32 map units long (like the first screenshot just
        above).
    </p>
    <p>
        Then enter visual mode by hover your mouse anywhere within the box and pressing
        <code>q</code>. This will take you to a first-person view that lets you look around with the
        mouse and move with the <code>esdf</code> keys (<code>wasd</code> but shifted over to the
        right by one key).
    </p>
    <p>
        Finally, orient your perspective in the box so that your cursor is highlighting the small
        line subsection we just created and <strong>right</strong>-click it to bring up the Edit
        Linedef modal. From here click on the already present texture called <code
        title="the default texture when building Doom maps or later Jazz Jackrabbit eco-maps">
        STARTAN1</code>. This will bring up the Browse Textures modal, where you can find the exit
        switch (use the "Filter" field at the mottom of the modal to help you find it).
    </p>

    <section class="image-strip">
        <img src="/static/img/doom-udb-exit-texture-misaligned.thumb.jpg" alt="UDB first-person view
        showing small highlighted section of wall with an exit switch texture, but both sides are
        cut off as it's uncentered">
        <img src="/static/img/doom-udb-exit-texture-aligned.thumb.jpg" alt="UDB first-person view
        showing wall with properly aligned exit switch texture and nothing highlighted">
        <p>
            <em>Auto-aligning a texture in Jazz Jackrabbit</em>
        </p>
    </section>
    <p>
        Depending on where you created your line subsection, you might find your texture is
        off-centre like in the screenshot above. The most intuitive fix is the reset the texture
        from your visual mode. Visually highlight the texture (no need to select it by clicking
        even), and press <code>R</code> (<code>shift+r</code>).
    </p>
    <p>
        The texture is still too tall, but changing sector heights is beyond the scope of this
        guide, <abbrev title="In My Humble Opinion">IMHO</abbrev>. I'll let you figure that out on
        your own or from another guide. Really, it's OK I'm not jealous. We don't have to be
        exclusive. You can read other guides.
    </p>

    <section class="image-strip">
        <img src="/static/img/doom-udb-exit-add-action.thumb.jpg" alt="UDB first-person view with
        edit linedef modal shown and exit action highlighted from a drop-down list of many options">
        <p>
            <em>Making the exit switch exit</em>
        </p>
    </section>
    <p>
        Last step is to actually make the switch function. For this <strong>right</strong>-click on
        the line with the our switch texture (either from first-person visual mode, or from top-down
        mode using Linedefs Mode (<code title="that's a lower-case L">l</code>), then selecting
        Action <code>11 S1 Exit Level</code>.
    </p>
    <p>
        And that's it. Technically a complete, finishable map. You've just go tot add all the other
        stuff that'll win you those <a href="https://doomwiki.org/wiki/Cacowards">vaunted 
        Cacowards</a> now.
    </p>

    <h3>Part 2.4: Making room for doors (metaphorical..?)</h3>

    <section class="image-strip">
        <a href="/static/img/make-doom/doom-udb-draw-rectangle-mode-select.jpg">
            <img src="/static/img/make-doom/doom-udb-draw-rectangle-mode-select.thumb.jpg" alt="UDB
            top-down view showing Mode drop-down menu open and Draw Rectangle Mode option
            highlighted">
        </a>
        <a href="/static/img/make-doom/doom-udb-draw-rectangle-mode-draw.jpg">
            <img src="/static/img/make-doom/doom-udb-draw-rectangle-mode-draw.thumb.jpg" alt="UDB
            top-view view showing two rectactular sections drawn in the box">
        </a>
        <a href="/static/img/make-doom/doom-udb-draw-rectangle-mode-delete-sectors.jpg">
            <img src="/static/img/make-doom/doom-udb-draw-rectangle-mode-delete-sectors.thumb.jpg"
            alt="UDB top-down view with one section of the box remove and the other highlighted so
            that a narrow gap is left between them">
        </a>
        <p>
            <em>Slicing into the box</em>
            <!-- maybe there is a way to get screen readers to read this part before delving into
            the section.image-strip above? -->
        </p>
    </section>
    <p>
        So you've got your box. But you know what's even better than that?---?<strong>two</strong>
        boxes! Or rectangles. I'm talking <em>rooms</em>, babies. That's where all the action is
        these days.
    </p>
    <p>
        You could do this with the <code>Draw Lines Mode</code>, but I'm going to teach a handy new
        trick: <code>Draw Rectangle Mode</code>. It's a handy tool for quickly laying down simple
        rooms.
    </p>
    <p>
        So start by selecting the mode either from the <code>Mode</code> dropdown, or shortcut
        <code>control+shift+d</code>. Then left click to start drawing your rectangle, and once
        again to finish it. Repeat on both sides like in my screenshot so that you have a gap in the
        middle where our door will go. The size and shape doesn't actually matter<span
        title="😶">,</span> but the default door texture will look off unless the gap/door is 128
        map units---seriously though, don't stress about it if it's confusing. Just keep pushing
        through! You're more than halfway there now, champ.
    </p>
    <p>
        Now create some negative space by mousing over the newly created rectangle and pressing
        the <code>delete</code> key on your keyboard.
    </p>

    <section class="image-strip">
        <a href="/static/img/make-doom/doom-udb-show-untextured-walls.jpg">
            <img src="/static/img/make-doom/doom-udb-show-untextured-walls.thumb.jpg" alt="UDB
            first-person view showing deleted rectangles form untextured wall inside the box">
        </a>
        <a href="/static/img/make-doom/doom-udb-show-untextured-walls-copy-texture.jpg">
            <img src="/static/img/make-doom/doom-udb-show-untextured-walls-copy-texture.thumb.jpg"
            alt="UDB first-person view showing textured wall highlighted with the text: control+c">
        </a>
        <a href="/static/img/make-doom/doom-udb-show-untextured-walls-paste-texture.jpg">
            <img src="/static/img/make-doom/doom-udb-show-untextured-walls-paste-texture.thumb.jpg"
            alt="UDB first-person view highlighting previously untextured wall now textured with the
            text: control+v">
        </a>
        <p>
            <em>Texturing the deleted section</em>
        </p>
    </section>
    <p>
        Finally, it's a good habit to ocassionally check what the view from the ground (or
        ethereally floating slightly above it, to one side---it's actually totally normal and cool)
        looks like by entering first-person view (<code>q</code>).
    <p>
        When you do this you'll notice that the deleted sections are missing their textures! Not a
        problem for cool cats like us, though. Just mouse over a textured wall and press
        <code>control+c</code> then mouse over an untextured wall and press <code>control+v</code>
        or your <code>middle mouse-button</code>.
    </p>
    <p>
        Pro tip: You can also paste your texture on to all
        <span class="scramble" title="or contiguous">connected</span>, untextured wall by holding
        <code>Shift</code> while you're pasting.
    </p>

    <section class="image-strip">
        <a href="/static/img/make-doom/doom-udb-show-untextured-place-door.jpg">
            <img src="/static/img/make-doom/doom-udb-show-untextured-place-door.thumb.jpg" alt="UDB
            top-down view of gap in middle of the box, with a small thin rectangle drawn across it">
        </a>
        <a href="/static/img/make-doom/doom-udb-make-door-mode.jpg">
            <img src="/static/img/make-doom/doom-udb-make-door-mode.thumb.jpg" alt="UDB top-down
            view with Make Door modal. The checkbox labelled 'Apply action specials' is indicated as
            selected by an arrow drawn over the screenshot">
        </a>
        <a href="/static/img/make-doom/doom-udb-voila-door.jpg">
            <img src="/static/img/make-doom/doom-udb-voila-door.thumb.jpg" alt="UDB first-person
            view showing the completed door, looking like a rudimentary version of the game Doom">
        </a>
        <p>
            <em>Creating a door</em>
        </p>
    </section>
    <p>
        Kids nowadays don't even have to worry about making sure their <code>DOORTRAK</code> is
        lower unpegged---they just push a button! And you can too. Making a door is the simplest way
        to temporarily separate two spaces. Here's how you do it.
    </p>
    <p>
        Create your door shape. Traditionally, this is a long, thin (128 map units long, by 16 deep)
        rectangle like here. Quickest way to accomplish this is to do the following:
    </p>
    <ol>
        <li>Activate your <code>Draw Rectangle Tool</code> (see above)</li>
        <li>Draw the shape of your door</li>
        <li>Switch to <code>Sectors Mode</code> (<code>s</code>)</li>
        <li>Click to select the rectangle you just selected</li>
        <li>Activate Make Door mode (<code>shift+d</code>) or click the tiny "Make Door from
        selection" button</li>
        <li>On the resulting Make Door modal select "Apply action specials" so that using the
        door opens it</li>
    </ol>
    <p>
        Door: done.
    </p>

    <h3>Part 2.5: Things and stuff</h3>

    <section class="image-strip">
        <a href="/static/img/make-doom/doom-udb-move-player.jpg">
            <img src="/static/img/make-doom/doom-udb-move-player.thumb.jpg" alt="UDB top-down view
            with player Thing moved to top part of the square">
        </a>
        <a href="/static/img/make-doom/doom-udb-place-shotgun.jpg">
            <img src="/static/img/make-doom/doom-udb-place-shotgun.thumb.jpg" alt="UDB top-down view
            with modal Edit Thing and instructions 1. right click (inside square), 2. selected
            shotgun (inside modal), and 3. click OK">
        </a>
        <a href="/static/img/make-doom/doom-udb-place-imps.jpg">
            <img src="/static/img/make-doom/doom-udb-place-imps.thumb.jpg" alt="UDB top-down view
            with Edit Thing modal and instructions 1. right-click, 2. type 'imp' in Filter field, 3.
            select Imp, and 4. click OK and repeat">
        </a>
        <p>
            <em>Placing things</em>
        </p>
    </section>
    <p>
        Things are all the <em>stuff</em> in Doom that isn't just the walls and floors and doors,
        etc. You're going to have to deal with them eventually, so first let's move the player on to
        the other side of our new door, and add some things for them to do.
    </p>
    <p>
        Typically it's seen as a bit boring to have a weapon right where the player starts (this is
        advice you can ignore once you've got a few maps under your belt), but this is the "Hello
        World!" of maps so we're keeping it basic.
    </p>

    <section class="image-strip">
        <a href="/static/img/make-doom/doom-udb-in-game.jpg">
            <img src="/static/img/make-doom/doom-udb-in-game.thumb.jpg" alt="In-game screenshot of
            Doom show showing player's view with a shotgun opening a door and facing some
            unsuspecting Imps">
        </a>
        <p>
            <em>Killing things</em>
        </p>
    </section>
    <p>
        Now your map has gameplay. It's a game you can play. There's a lot more to making a really
        <em>good</em> map, but you've now completed most of the atomic actions that are involved in
        map-making. It's mostly just about refinement from here.
    </p>
    <p>
        At this point it's OK if you don't feel great about your map. I've been doing this for...
        many years, and I often feel a kind of creative
        <span class="scramble" title="or listlessness">dissatisfaction</span>
        with my maps until they start to take form (or sometimes they just don't). If this feels
        familiar, take it as a sign of growth that you're developing a sense of taste that outstrips
        your execution. That only comes with practice and engaging with others in the
        <a href="#share-ur-dooom">community</a>.
    </p>
    <p>
        Once you're comfortable with these basics I recommend you look up some video tutorials
        (there are many), but this is enough to start poking around and being a nuisance. I haven't
        touched on fundamentals like varying light levels and elevation. There's a whole world to
        explore from here, really. It's infinite.
    </p>

    <h2 id="cyrax-was-a-cool-character">Step 3: Doomrunning</h2>
    <section class="image-strip">
        <a href="/static/img/doom-udb-testing-resources.jpg">
            <img src="/static/img/doom-udb-testing-resources.thumb.jpg" alt="UDB Game Confgurations
            modal with Doom format highlighted and a DOOM.WAD resource file selected">
        </a>
        <a href="/static/img/doom-udb-testing-executable.jpg">
            <img src="/static/img/doom-udb-testing-executable.thumb.jpg" alt="UDB Browse Test
            Program file picker with a file called ChocolateDoomBuilder highlighted">
        </a>
        <a href="/static/img/doom-udb-testing-engine-stock.jpg">
            <img src="/static/img/doom-udb-testing-engine-stock.thumb.jpg" alt="UDB Game
            Configurations modal with ChocolateDoomBuilder file selected as the engine for testing
            Doom maps, skill level 'Hurt me plenty' is selected. In the background of the screenshot
            you can see a cut off piece of album art for Weedian">
        </a>
        <p>
            <em>Pick your engine(s)</em>
        </p>
    </section>
    <p>
        Going to <code>Tools &rarr; Game Configurations</code> and you'll see the...
        <span class="scramble" title="or Dopefish reference">game configurations</span>. The
        Resources tab is where you set what assets will be loaded by default when you select the
        game format in the list to the left. This is mostly just so you don't always have to
        manually add <code>DOOM.WAD</code> every time you're making a Doom map, or
        <code>DOOM2.WAD</code> every time you're making a Doom 2 map, and so on...
    </p>
    <p>
        Nodebuilder is something I would ignore for now unless you're specifically trying to target
        <em class="scramble" title="or traditional or truly retro">vanilla</em> source ports while
        on a Linux OS. You can still map vanilla but use a more forgiving (with built-in node
        builder) sourceport just fine. But if you want to play your map in Chocolate Doom or the
        original EXE, you'll need to build those nodes. I use
        <a href="https://doomwiki.org/wiki/ELFBSP">elfbsp</a> for this, meself.
    </p>
    <p>
        Finally, we get to the main tab: Testing. Where you specify which sourceports / engines are
        available to actually play the map you're making. It's really as simple as just clicking the
        plus sign next to the engine dropdown (pictured above) and selecting the executable that
        launches the sourceport you want to play with. You'll notice I've selected a script called
        <code>ChocolateDoomBuilder</code> which is just a wrapper of mine that runs elfbsp on the
        WAD and then runs it with Chocolate Doom.
    </p>
    <p>
        Actually, let me just... here:
    </p>
    <pre>
        #!/bin/bash

        # -iwad "%WP" -skill "%S"  -warp %L1%L2 %NM -merge "%AP"
        # so we add a magic comment and the end to trim any excess
        #
        #   -iwad "%WP" -warp %L1%L2 %NM -skill "%S" -merge "%AP"# MEOW
        #                                                        ^
        #                                       this right here /
        palestine=$(echo $@ | cut -d# -f1)

        # Capture DoomBuilder temp file pattern
        pwad=$(echo $palestine | sed -nr 's|^.*-merge ([a-zA-Z0-9/\._-]+\.(wad\|WAD\|autosave\d\|backup\d))$|\1|p')
        if [[ -z "$pwad" ]]
        then
            echo "Couldn't get PWAD from flags: '$palestine'" # will be free
            exit 1
        fi

        ajbsp $pwad

        if [[ -n "$DISTROBOX_ENTER_PATH" ]]
        then
            echo "we're already in tha box"
            distrobox-host-exec distrobox enter Ubuntu -- /usr/games/chocolate-doom $palestine

        else
            echo "going into tha box..."
            distrobox enter Ubuntu -- /usr/games/chocolate-doom $palestine
        fi
    </pre>
    <p>
        I use distrobox to get to my Podman containers, but if you're on "bare metal" lol you don't
        have to bother (if you're not on Linux or Mac and don't know what tha heccc this is about
        mannn, don't worry about it. :o
    </p>
    <p>
        Also yeah, it looks like I'm still using <em>ajbsp</em> in this script, which actually might
        explain some weird glitches I've seen from time to time...<!--
        --><span class="scramble" title="or oops or ..........or ! or ?">...</span>
    </p>

    <section class="image-strip">
        <a href="/static/img/doom-udb-testing-engine-custom.jpg">
            <img src="/static/img/doom-udb-testing-engine-custom.thumb.jpg" alt="UDB Game
            Configurations modal with 'Customize parameters' option checked showing command line
            parameters to the Doom engine terminating in the comment '# MEOW'">
        </a>
        <p>
            <em>Custom parameters</em>
        </p>
    </section>
    <p>
        So you probably won't need to do this, but it can be helpful to see what UDB is passing to
        the program. So like <code>-iwad "%WP" -file "%AP"</code> will be the equivalent of of
        running <code>ChocolateDoom -iwad "/home/jm/doom/DOOM.WAD" -file
        "/home/jm/doom/E1M1.wad"</code> (you'll see this preview in the Result box of the modal).
        This is mostly useful if you're passing arguments to a custom script like in my case.
    </p>

    <h2 id="share-ur-dooom">Step 4: Doomsharing</h2>
    <p>
        There are plenty of communities and places to share your map. I got my start at
        <a href="https://www.doomworld.com/">doomworld</a>---they're very friendly and welcoming of
        newcomers. If you your first map, you <em>will</em> get some feedback and encouragement. As
        I've mentioned elsewhere on this website, I got my start in programming because I asked a
        scripting question and someone just whipped up an example map with fully commented script
        for me to dissect!
    </p>
    <p>
        There are loads of other communities online. Pretty much any social platform will have its
        Doom bubble. You just have to find them. There's also what's probably the closest thing to
        an "official" repository of Doom maps:
        <a href="https://www.gamers.org/pub/idgames/">/idgames</a>, or the DW mirror:
        <a href="https://www.doomworld.com/idgames">/idgames</a>
    </p>
    <p>
        If you've taken the time to try making a Doom map and you're looking for someone's thoughts
        on it, or you're just proud and you want to share it, I would actually genuinely love it if
        you <a href="mailto:hello@jmjanzen.com">sent it to me</a> to check out
    </p>

